The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue and one queued fix.
The exported logic shows two distinct causes. We split them into a
[parser issue](https://github.com/mlschodkowski/mega-dev-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1) and a
[booking-form hint issue](https://github.com/mlschodkowski/mega-dev-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2).

## Engineering update: 5 October 2026

The [course's engineering update](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows#engineering-update)
reports #4471 closed on 5 October:

- **Parser: fixed.** Engineering reports `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
  Close the parser issue as completed.
- **Hint: won't fix.** Engineering retired the booking-v3 form.
  Close the hint issue as not planned. Retirement is not a code fix for the hint.

These are reported engineering outcomes. We have not inspected a production deployment.
The historical export counts below remain useful even after the tickets close.

<Reassessment />

## Check the supplied classification

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

Tenant locale comes from the tenant directory. Use `Intl.DateTimeFormat` to determine
the date order. For a day-first tenant, a `form` report through `booking-v3` belongs to
`hint`; other channels belong to `parser`. Browser locale and `meta.intakeTag` are intake
guesses. They do not establish the tenant's date order or the cause.

The parser reads month first. For example, a day-first date `07/04/2026` for 7 April
can become 4 July. The separate booking-v3 problem prints `MM/DD/YYYY` while its form
reader uses the tenant's day-first order. Each needs its own fix and its own ticket.

